        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "62ec42d7-4cf0-5d50-9fb0-ecc80349657b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Crepis, Isatis, Sinapis, Aethionema, Iberis, Biscutella\n- People: E. B. Babcock\n- Animals: Euchloe simplonia\n- Concepts: Asteraceae, Brassicaceae, polyploidy, apomixis\n\nFacts:\n- Crepis is a genus of annual and perennial flowering plants in the family Asteraceae.\n- Crepis is commonly called hawksbeard or hawk's-beard in some regions.\n- Crepis superficially resembles the dandelion.\n- Crepis usually has branching scapes with multiple flower heads, although solitary heads can also occur.\n- Isatis is a genus of about 30 species of flowering plants in the family Brassicaceae.\n- Isatis is native from the Mediterranean region east to central Asia.\n- E. B. Babcock is particularly known for studying the entire polyploid apomictic genus Crepis through field investigations and extensive experiments.\n- E. B. Babcock recognized 196 species within Crepis.\n- Euchloe simplonia mainly feeds on species of Sinapis, Isatis, Aethionema, Iberis, and Biscutella.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a3f37ece-35bd-50dd-a3dc-dfcaaa924832",
        "text": "Page: Crepis. Sentence: Crepis, commonly known in some parts of the world as hawksbeard or hawk's-beard (but not to be confused with the related genus \"Hieracium\" with a similar common name), is a genus of annual and perennial flowering plants of the family Asteraceae superficially resembling the dandelion, the most conspicuous difference being that \"Crepis\" usually has branching scapes with multiple heads (though solitary heads can occur).\nPage: Isatis. Sentence: Isatis is a genus of about 30 species of flowering plants in the family Brassicaceae, native to the Mediterranean region east to central Asia.\nPage: E. B. Babcock. Sentence: He is particularly known for seeking to understand by field investigations and extensive experiments, the entire polyploid apomictic genus \"Crepis\", in which he recognize 196 species.\nPage: Euchloe simplonia. Sentence: Its main foods are \"Sinapis\", \"Isatis\", \"Aethionema\", \"Iberis\" and \"Biscutella\" species.",
